## Reusing a problem in another chapter {#sec-anatomy-reuse}

Every solved problem is written **once**, in the problem registry (`books/problems/<key>/`), and included in full by its home chapter. When a later chapter needs it again — to compare it with a variant, or to remind the reader of an idea — it includes the problem's **card** instead. This is the card for Climbing Stairs, included here exactly as a chapter would:

<!-- Generated by scripts/gen_book.py from problem.qmd. Do not edit. -->

::: {.problem-card}

[Problem]{.card-kicker} **Climbing stairs** [LC 70 · Climbing Stairs](https://leetcode.com/problems/climbing-stairs/){difficulty="Easy"}

Look at the **last** step of any climb to step $i$: it is either a 1-step from $i-1$ or a 2-step from $i-2$. These two groups do not overlap and together contain every climb, so the counts add.

[Solved in full in @sec-dpi-p1-stairs.]{.card-link}

:::


The card is generated from the problem itself (its title, source and intuition), so it never goes out of date, and its link always points to the one full write-up. A chapter can also include the problem's `solution.qmd` to run the same tested code again.

## How to write a new chapter

```bash
python scripts/new_chapter.py NNN --prefix SLUG                          # from outline.yml + the template
python scripts/new_problem.py LC 70 "Climbing Stairs" --difficulty Easy  # one registry folder per problem
.venv/Scripts/python scripts/check_chapter.py books/<folder>/NNN_<slug>.qmd
.venv/Scripts/python scripts/check_links.py --online
powershell scripts/build.ps1
```

The build adds the chapter to the sidebar, marks it as written on the home page, generates the cards of its problems, and, for chapters from Part II on, adds its problems to the problem index (@sec-problem-index).
